&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-fallback" data-lang="fallback"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;Hey folks! Just 3 parts for you to place in your miniatures to customize your characters. Extra thick to increase compatibility. Heavily based in historic milanese armor (high waist large shoulder piece, few folds)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;My process to adapt them (blender 2.8+):
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;1- Load your miniature and the armor parts
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;2- Place them more or less in position
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;3- Get the best fit you can simply scaling it (if some parts of the mini stick out, don&amp;#39;t worry, get the overall placement)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;4 - Try sculpting down (elastic deform brush in blender works wonders!) the parts sticking out in the original mini.
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;5- Use the sculpting elastic deform to give the final touches.
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;It normally takes less than 5 min for even somewhat complex models!
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;Hope you enjoy it!
